//@version=5
// Force bar — paints the candles the tape had to work for.
//
// Three rulers, three answers, one candle. A bar wears at most one colour,
// and the priority is fixed: biggest > exhaustion > force.
//
// HOW TO READ THE CHART:
//
//   yellow / orange   BIGGEST BAR — its range (high-low) equals the widest
//                     of the last N bars. The window's own extreme; it says
//                     nothing about the body.
//   aqua / purple     EXHAUSTION — body more than `factor` times the average
//                     body of the bars BEFORE it. The move that overreached.
//   lime / red        FORCE — body between the min and max multiples of the
//                     average body. Deliberately a *band*: a bar bigger than
//                     the max is no longer force, it is exhaustion, and the
//                     two never wear the same colour.
//   no colour         An ordinary bar. Most bars are ordinary, and a chart
//                     where everything is painted has told you nothing.
//
// The bright colour of each pair is the bullish bar (close >= open), the
// darker one the bearish bar.
//
// Two rulers, not one: force and exhaustion measure the BODY (|close-open|,
// what the tape actually carried); biggest measures the RANGE (high-low,
// what it reached and gave back). That is why the third mark is not a louder
// version of the first two — a long wick with no body is the widest bar of
// the window and no force at all.
//
// Exhaustion compares against `body[1]` — the average of the bars *before*
// this one — so a huge bar cannot inflate the average that is judging it.
// Force compares against the average including itself, which is the milder,
// more forgiving test of the two, on purpose.
//
// HONEST ABOUT THE FORMING BAR: the bar being built is painted from its
// preview and may change colour — or lose it — until it closes. That is the
// state of an unfinished bar, not a promise about the closed one. Once a bar
// commits, its colour never changes again.
//
// Warm-up: no bar is painted before its rulers have history — the averages
// need their window, and the biggest-bar test needs N bars to compare. An
// unpainted opening stretch is the indicator saying it does not know yet.
//
// Ties count: "biggest" is >= , so if several bars in the window share the
// widest range they are all painted. On a flat, low-activity stretch where
// bars come out the same size that can colour a run of them at once — the
// honest reading of "nothing here stands out", not a signal each time.
//
// (The TradingView original used fuchsia for bearish exhaustion; this
// dialect's palette has no fuchsia, so purple stands in for it.)
indicator("Force bar", overlay=true)

// 1 — force: the band of "worked for it" bodies.
show_force = input.bool(true, "1 Force: paint force bars")
force_len = input.int(20, "1 Force: average body window (bars)", minval=1, maxval=500)
force_min = input.float(1.5, "1 Force: min body (×average)", minval=0.1, maxval=20.0, step=0.1)
force_max = input.float(2.5, "1 Force: max body (×average)", minval=0.1, maxval=20.0, step=0.1)
force_bull = input.color(color.lime, "1 Force: bullish colour")
force_bear = input.color(color.red, "1 Force: bearish colour")

// 2 — exhaustion: past the band, measured against the bars before it.
show_exhaustion = input.bool(true, "2 Exhaustion: paint exhaustion bars")
exhaustion_len = input.int(20, "2 Exhaustion: average body window (bars)", minval=1, maxval=500)
exhaustion_factor = input.float(2.5, "2 Exhaustion: min body (×average)", minval=0.1, maxval=20.0, step=0.1)
exhaustion_bull = input.color(color.aqua, "2 Exhaustion: bullish colour")
exhaustion_bear = input.color(color.purple, "2 Exhaustion: bearish colour")

// 3 — biggest: the widest range of the window.
show_biggest = input.bool(true, "3 Biggest: paint the widest bar")
biggest_len = input.int(20, "3 Biggest: window (bars)", minval=2, maxval=500)
biggest_bull = input.color(color.yellow, "3 Biggest: bullish colour")
biggest_bear = input.color(color.orange, "3 Biggest: bearish colour")

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- measures

body = math.abs(close - open)
bar_range = high - low

// Every kernel is hoisted to the top level and fed on every bar, whatever the
// toggles say: a `ta.*` gated behind a display switch would carry a different
// history the moment the trader flipped it, and the same tape would then
// classify differently depending on when a checkbox was clicked.
avg_body = ta.sma(body, force_len)
avg_body_before = ta.sma(body[1], exhaustion_len)
widest_range = ta.highest(bar_range, biggest_len)

// The two multiples are read in whichever order they were typed; a min above
// the max would otherwise silently mean "never".
force_lo = math.min(force_min, force_max)
force_hi = math.max(force_min, force_max)

// A zero average is warm-up (or a dead tape): na, not a division by zero, and
// `not na(...)` below is what keeps those bars unpainted.
force_ratio = avg_body > 0 ? body / avg_body : na
exhaustion_ratio = avg_body_before > 0 ? body / avg_body_before : na

// The widest-of-N test is meaningless until N bars exist: without this every
// one of the first bars is trivially the widest so far.
has_window = bar_index >= biggest_len - 1

is_force = show_force and not na(force_ratio) and force_ratio >= force_lo and force_ratio <= force_hi
is_exhaustion = show_exhaustion and not na(exhaustion_ratio) and exhaustion_ratio > exhaustion_factor
is_biggest = show_biggest and has_window and bar_range >= widest_range

bullish = close >= open

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ paint

// One colour per bar, decided in priority order. A bar that matches nothing
// falls through with `na` and keeps the chart's own candle colours.
paint = na
if is_biggest
    paint := bullish ? biggest_bull : biggest_bear
else if is_exhaustion
    paint := bullish ? exhaustion_bull : exhaustion_bear
else if is_force
    paint := bullish ? force_bull : force_bear

barcolor(paint)
